NNAAMMEE
     aauuddiitt — audit management utility

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aauuddiitt --ee | --ii | --nn | --ss | --tt

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aauuddiitt utility controls the state of the audit system.  One of the
     following flags is required as an argument to aauuddiitt:

     --ee      Forces the audit system to immediately remove audit log files
             that meet the expiration criteria specified in the audit control
             file without doing a log rotation.

     --ii      Initializes and starts auditing.  This option is currently for
             Mac OS X only and requires auditd(8) to be configured to run un‐
             der launchd(8).

     --nn      Forces the audit system to close the existing audit log file and
             rotate to a new log file in a location specified in the audit
             control file.  Also, audit log files that meet the expiration
             criteria specified in the audit control file will be removed.

     --ss      Specifies that the audit system should [re]synchronize its con‐
             figuration from the audit control file.  A new log file will be
             created.

     --tt      Specifies that the audit system should terminate.  Log files are
             closed and renamed to indicate the time of the shutdown.

NNOOTTEESS
     The auditd(8) daemon must already be running.  Optionally, it can be con‐
     figured to be started on-demand by launchd(8) (Mac OS X only).  The aauuddiitt
     utility requires audit administrator privileges for successful operation.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/security/audit_control  Audit policy file used to configure the au‐
                                  diting system.

